= The `desmet` Harness <sec-harness>

#include "../figures/harness-architecture.typ"

== Architecture

The harness is a Python package whose components are shown in @fig-arch. A *runner* iterates over the selected platforms and scenarios, instantiates each platform's adapter through a registry, invokes the four stage methods in sequence, and collects a typed `StageResult` per stage. A *scenario loader* validates YAML scenario definitions and builds the `StageContext` (scenario text, acceptance criteria, prior-stage outputs, budgets) that every stage receives. A *metrics collector* turns each stage's trace into framework metrics and the cross-cutting scores of @sec-framework, and a DuckDB *result store* persists run metadata and per-execution records alongside per-stage JSON and trace files on disk. Containerised platforms and the Langfuse tracing service are managed through Docker Compose profiles by an *infrastructure* module. A management console (FastAPI backend, Svelte front end) launches runs, streams logs, presents the rubric form beside the Langfuse span tree, a LangSmith run tree where available, and a directed graph of inter-agent messages reconstructed from the trace, and renders cross-platform comparisons; it is the instrument through which the manual rubric judgements are made, and it shares the result store with the command-line interface.

== Adapter contract

Every platform is integrated through a subclass of `BasePlatformAdapter`, which declares one method per stage: `generate_requirements`, `generate_code`, `generate_tests`, and `build_and_deploy`. Programmatic platforms (multi-agent frameworks and SDK runtimes) extend `ToolAgentAdapter`, which implements all four stage methods through a shared template---prompt construction from the scenario and prior results, tool creation, trace lifecycle, retry orchestration, and result building---and leaves a single platform-specific method, `_run_agent`, for the subclass. Visual platforms that execute behind a REST API extend a parallel `VisualAgentAdapter`, which provides the analogous retry loop for flows built programmatically from each platform's live component catalogue. Adding a programmatic platform therefore means implementing `_run_agent`, registering the class, and adding a platform entry to the configuration file; the runner, metrics, rubric, and console are untouched.

== Stage execution pattern

Within `ToolAgentAdapter` each stage runs the same three-role pattern. A *planner* produces an `ImplementationPlan`, a Pydantic-validated structure of steps and target files, with a plain-text fallback for models whose structured-output fidelity is weak. An *executor* receives instructions enriched with the parsed plan and target files and performs the work with write-capable tools. A *reviewer* validates the workspace under an asymmetric tool distribution---inspection tools only, no write access---and if validation fails the executor is re-run with the reviewer's feedback appended to its history, up to the scenario's iteration budget. After every stage the base class audits the workspace for out-of-scope file modifications.

Stage-specific tools are injected by the template. Stage~1 renders Mermaid sources through the Mermaid CLI so that downstream reviewers can inspect the diagrams. Stage~2 writes into a workspace initialised from a minimal baseline project. Stage~3 adds `run_tests` (pytest or jest) inside an error-recovery loop, so the agent can iterate on failing tests. Stage~4 exposes a single `deploy_remote` tool with three actions---`push` (commit and push the workspace to a per-run branch), `restart` (SSH to the target, pull, `docker compose up --build`), and `health_check` (SSH and probe the service endpoint)---and gives the agent no CI system to delegate to. Targeting a remote host is a deliberate measurement choice: a local deploy collapses into a single tool invocation, whereas push--restart--probe forces the multi-step sequencing that the _Orchestration_ score is designed to capture, and calling `health_check` unprompted is the clearest observable act of autonomy in the pipeline. The target runs a restricted user with a whitelisted shell and network-restricted SSH access.

== Metrics and observability

Token usage is captured per LLM call by an observation collector wrapped around each stage, fed by an adapter-specific source: OpenAI-compatible adapters read the usage fields of each response, CrewAI uses a native completion client with an interceptor registered on its event bus (its earlier `litellm`-based path was removed upstream in v1.6), and Microsoft Agent Framework inserts a usage-tracking middleware into its chat pipeline. Wall-clock time is recorded at stage boundaries by the runner, cost is derived from token counts and provider price tables, and peak memory is sampled from the host process. Every LLM and tool call is also emitted as a Langfuse span, which both the console and the trace audit consume. When a run is configured with repeats, the collector additionally produces `VarianceMetrics` (mean and population standard deviation of tokens, time, cost, tool calls, and iterations) so that stability under non-deterministic outputs can be reported.

== Hybrid scoring and the productive-tool-call guard

Of the four cross-cutting scores, _Pipeline Completeness_, _Efficiency_, and _Orchestration_ are derived automatically; _Autonomy_ is scored by the evaluator in the console and optionally blended 50/50 with the logged intervention count when an adapter records one. _Pipeline Completeness_ is seeded from each stage's success flag---and that flag is where a naive validator misleads. The harness originally accepted a stage as successful when the files the stage was expected to produce existed; because later stages inherit the workspace, baseline files produced earlier can satisfy that check even when the agent did nothing. A *productive-tool-call guard* now demotes a stage to failure when no `write_file`, `execute_shell`, or `deploy_remote` call occurred during it. The guard fires on real cases (@sec-results): a run whose every stage failed with zero tool calls would otherwise have reported three stages passed.

== Parity versus idiomatic use

A controlled comparison needs shared structure, but a comparison that forced every platform through one orchestration shape would measure the adapter rather than the platform. The template holds prompts, tools, retry policy, and scoring constant; `_run_agent` uses each platform's native primitives. LangGraph compiles a three-node `StateGraph` (planner, executor, reviewer) with in-memory checkpointing and a conditional edge from reviewer back to executor. CrewAI builds a sequential crew of role-based agents with a `check_completion` tool marked as the final answer, which terminates the crew when the reviewer signals pass and prevents the runaway loop that otherwise exhausts the iteration budget. OpenAI Agents SDK chains three agents by handoff with an output guardrail on the reviewer that triggers retry. Google ADK composes a `SequentialAgent` whose second element is a `LoopAgent` over executor and reviewer, exited through the native `exit_loop` tool. Microsoft Agent Framework constructs a Magentic manager-driven team, capped by the harness at four manager rounds and two stalls to bound worst-case cost. A structured audit of the five adapters removed several measurement defects (silently dropped events, cumulative iteration counters, double-counted token and duration layers, a swallowed planner fallback) before the reported runs; three comparability gaps remain and are stated in @sec-threats.
